module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.append_only_carrier_whose_serialization_shares_a_merge_region

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data append_only_carrier_whose_serialization_shares_a_merge_region: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "append_only_carrier_whose_serialization_shares_a_merge_region" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a carrier that every lane is INSTRUCTED to append to, serialized so that independent appends CAN LAND IN THE SAME MERGE REGION AND NOTHING DECIDES WHETHER THEY DO, makes collision a standing property of the carrier rather than of any disagreement** (INVALID STATE: two lanes add logically disjoint content -- different rows, different subjects, no shared meaning -- and the textual merge refuses, because the serialization put their edits on the same line or at the same end of the same list. **THE CONDITIONAL IS STATED AS A CAPABILITY AND NOT AS A CERTAINTY, BECAUSE THE TWO ARE DIFFERENT CLAIMS AND AN EARLIER REVISION CONFLATED THEM.** WHERE two appends actually occupy one region their collision is NECESSARY, not merely likely; what varies is WHETHER they land there, and nothing in the carrier or the process decides that. So the invalid state is the CAPABILITY -- a serialization under which two disjoint appends may share a region -- and the frequency is a separate, measured claim about how often that capability is exercised. Stating the antecedent as 'occupy the same region' and the consequent as 'default' asserted certainty and likelihood of the same fact at once, which is why it read as internally inconsistent beside the counterexample below.  Nothing is contested. The authors do not disagree about anything. The conflict is manufactured entirely by the layout of the bytes. **A CLEAN MERGE OF THIS CARRIER IS NOT EVIDENCE AGAINST THE CLASS** -- stated first because the reader who most needs it is the one who stops reading early, and the next person to see a roster merge cleanly will otherwise cite it as a refutation. HARM: it is paid by every lane, on every append, forever, and it is paid in the most expensive currency available -- a human or agent hand-resolving a merge whose correct answer is mechanically determined. Worse, hand-resolution of a mechanical union is where REAL content gets dropped, because the resolver is pattern-matching rather than reading.",

    "TWO CARRIERS, ONE MECHANISM, AND THEY ARE FILED AS ONE ROW BECAUSE ONE CAPABILITY RETIRES BOTH. `gunbc.rung_drop` rows serialize as multi-kilobyte SINGLE-LINE blobs, so two lanes editing disjoint FIELDS OF ONE ROW conflict on that line -- three lanes hit it in one night. `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.roster` is an append-only list with a PARALLEL import block, so two lanes appending different rows conflict twice, once per block. Different shapes -- one row too wide, one list too append-hot -- and the same defect: THE UNIT OF MERGE IS COARSER THAN THE UNIT OF EDIT. Specimens unify on mechanism and never on symptom, and the test that these are one class is that a serialization in which independent appends do not share a merge region retires both at once.",

    "RECEIPT: THREE BRANCHES OF ONE SESSION CONFLICTED ON THE ROSTER SIMULTANEOUSLY, WITHIN EIGHT MINUTES, ON THE SAME TWO BLOCKS. Main had appended two rows; each branch had appended its own. The resolution was a union in identical order in both blocks and was mechanical in all three -- no judgement was exercised, and none was available to exercise. That is the diagnostic signature: if every resolution of a conflict class is the same mechanical operation, the conflict carried no information and the merge was asking a question with a computable answer.",

    "THE RESOLUTION HAS ITS OWN TRAP, AND A SET-GRAIN CHECK WALKS INTO IT. Roster ORDER is load-bearing in the generated projection, so the union must agree as an ORDERED SEQUENCE and not merely as a set -- a reordering changes the emitted bytes while every membership test stays green. The verification that does work is diffing the import block against the roster list as sequences; the verification that looks equivalent and is not is comparing their lengths or their sets.",

    "DISTINCT FROM THE GENERATED-PROJECTION CONFLICT THAT ACCOMPANIES IT, AND THE BOUNDARY DECIDES THE REMEDY. When both sides change a generated artifact, NEITHER side's bytes are the projection of the merged authorities, so the correct answer is a regeneration and a hand-resolution is definitionally wrong. Here both sides' bytes are AUTHORED and both are correct; the union IS the answer and the only defect is that a machine is not computing it. One class must refuse resolution, the other must not require it.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE: FOR EVERY CARRIER THE PROCESS INSTRUCTS LANES TO APPEND TO, ASK WHETHER TWO INDEPENDENT APPENDS SHARE A MERGE REGION. If they do, the conflict rate is a function of LANE CONCURRENCY and not of disagreement, so it rises with adoption -- the carrier gets worse exactly as the practice it supports succeeds. The tell is a conflict whose every historical resolution was the same mechanical union, and the sharper tell is a carrier whose instructions tell every lane to write to one place while its serialization assumes one writer.",

    "SPECIMEN REQUIRING NO SECOND AUTHOR AND NO DISAGREEMENT (2026-09-04, recurring_failure_mode roster). One author held three appends to this roster's single region across two of their OWN open heads: #10417 adding one row, and #10436 adding two after a class was split in review. The two appends sharing #10436's head do not collide with each other; the collision is between the two heads. Nobody disagreed about anything and there was no second party to coordinate with -- every participant was the same author -- and the enumeration collided regardless. ",

    "WHAT THAT SPECIMEN DOES AND DOES NOT ESTABLISH, stated because the tempting stronger claim is false. It does NOT show a nonzero conflict floor at concurrency one: the causal variable in the recognition rule above is INDEPENDENT APPENDS, which is a count of HEADS diverging from one base, and git neither knows nor cares that one person authored both -- so this specimen is concurrency two under the only reading the mechanism supports, and an author count of one is not a concurrency of one. What it DOES establish is that the collision needs no disagreement and no second party, so coordination has nothing here to arbitrate. That is what distinguishes a serialization defect from contention, and it is the reading this row should be cited for.",

    "**A COUNTEREXAMPLE ARRIVED FROM THIS ROW'S OWN LANDING AND IT NARROWS THE CLAIM, SO IT IS RECORDED HERE RATHER THAN LEFT FOR A READER TO DISCOVER.** An earlier revision of this row said the conflict was a CERTAINTY. It is not. Within the hour of this row merging, a sibling branch of the same session merged main carrying two fresh roster appends and the roster MERGED CLEANLY -- only the generated projection refused. Git's three-way merge resolves two appends whenever they are separated by enough unchanged context; it refuses only when both land at the same insertion point, which is what happened to three branches earlier the same night and is what a strictly append-at-the-end discipline produces. **SO THE HONEST CLAIM IS ABOUT THE DEFAULT AND NOT ABOUT NECESSITY: the collision is a function of whether two appends choose the same insertion point, which nothing coordinates and nothing forbids** -- which is worse than a certainty for diagnosis, because an intermittent mechanical conflict is one people learn to resolve by reflex instead of ranking for repair. The capability in the trigger is unchanged by this: a serialization where independent appends do not share a merge region removes the possibility rather than lowering its rate. **AND THE NARROWING APPLIES TO ONE CARRIER AND NOT THE OTHER, WHICH IS A DISTINCTION WORTH CARRYING RATHER THAN A QUALIFICATION.** The counterexample is about INSERTION POINTS in a list, so it narrows the roster carrier, where two appends may or may not land together. It does NOT narrow the rung_drop case, because there the colliding edits are disjoint FIELDS OF ONE ROW and that row is a single line -- rows still reach 63,174 characters on one line after the per-file split -- so there is no second insertion point for two edits to differ on. **A SHARED LINE ADMITS NO SEPARATION, SO WITHIN A ROW THE COLLISION REMAINS A CERTAINTY WHILE ACROSS A LIST IT IS ONLY THE DEFAULT.** Note also that the per-file split has already landed, so two lanes declaring DIFFERENT drops now write different files and no longer collide at all; what survives is two lanes editing one drop. That is the same capability from the other side: the split separated rows and left fields sharing a region. **AND THAT MAKES rung_drop A WORKED EXAMPLE, IN ONE CARRIER'S OWN HISTORY, OF A PARTIAL FIX SATISFYING THE VISIBLE HALF OF A CAPABILITY WHILE THE OTHER HALF STAYS DEAD** -- which is the §4b(3) shape this trigger was written to resist, demonstrated by the very carrier the trigger is about. The split was real work and it removed a real collision; it is also the artifact that would have been cited to retire this trigger, and every field-level collision would have survived it. The receipt is that this row's own author read the repairing commit as evidence of a live defect and had to be asked before checking -- so the half that got fixed became the visible one and the half that remained became invisible, to the person writing the row about it. **A TRIGGER THAT CAN POINT AT ITS OWN CARRIER'S HISTORY AS THE FAILURE MODE IT PREVENTS IS HARDER TO RETIRE BY AN ARTIFACT THAN ONE THAT ONLY ASSERTS THE RISK.** RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable, and the mitigation is that a resolver reads both sides and unions them by hand. CEILING: structurally impossible -- a serialization in which an independent append occupies its own merge region admits no textual collision between disjoint appends, so the invalid state has no constructor rather than being caught by a check. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, a CAPABILITY and not either carrier's cleanup: independent appends to a shared carrier do not share a merge region, AND the carrier's derived order is computed rather than authored, so that membership and order cannot disagree. Splitting rung_drop into one row per file, or reformatting the roster, satisfies neither half and leaves every other append-hot carrier in the tree exposed in the same way.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
